              "url": "https://docs.consensus.canister.software/join/#start"
            },
            {
              "@type": "HowToStep",
              "position": 2,
              "name": "Run guided setup",
              "text": "One command installs the approved release, runs the live benchmark, verifies email, collects payout addresses, and registers. A domain is issued on pass.",
              "url": "https://docs.consensus.canister.software/join/#start"
            },
            {
              "@type": "HowToStep",
              "position": 3,
              "name": "Keep it running",
              "text": "Start the always-on control tunnel under pm2 and save it, so the node survives reboots and stays in the active set.",
              "url": "https://docs.consensus.canister.software/join/#start"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "@type": "FAQPage",
          "@id": "https://docs.consensus.canister.software/join/#faq",
          "mainEntity": [
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "name": "What does a Consensus node actually do?",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "A node carries live traffic for the network. It forwards HTTP and TCP tunnels so local services reach a public HTTPS URL, executes deduplicated proxy requests as a forward or reverse proxy, offers its static IPv4 address to apps that need a whitelisted IP, and hosts prepaid, metered WebSocket sessions."
              }
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "name": "Do nodes earn rewards during the beta?",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "No. No rewards are paid during the public beta, and the network is free to use. The beta exists to expand the network and find viable nodes: machines that pass admission and keep passing the heartbeat and re-tests under real traffic. Registration is complete, payout addresses included, so a node that joins now is fully set up once the reward model is decided."
              }
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "name": "What hardware do I need to run a node?",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "A machine with at least 8 GB of memory, 4 cores, 50 GB of SSD storage, and 100 Mbps of bandwidth, plus one static public IPv4 address with a single open inbound port. The node runs on Bun and needs to stay online 24/7."
              }
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "name": "How does admission work?",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "After the baseline hardware check, the machine runs a live stress test: at least 5000 hash/s encryption speed, a sustained 60 seconds at 16 KB, p99 responsiveness under 10 ms, and packet loss below 2%. Admission is not permanent — a heartbeat every five minutes proves availability and the network re-tests nodes during their downtime."
              }
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "name": "How long does setup take?",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Three commands. Clone the node runtime and install it with Bun, run the guided setup that installs the approved release, runs the benchmark, verifies your email, collects payout addresses, and registers the node, then start the control tunnel under pm2 so it survives reboots. A domain is issued once the node passes."
              }
            }
          ]
        }
